.792, 44.827, 44.873, 44.897, 44.92, 44.943, 44.978, 45.024, 45.071, 45.129, 45.175, 45.222, 45.303, 45.431, 45.709, 45.767, 45.814, 45.849, 45.907, 45.93, 45.953, 45.976, 46.023, 46.069, 46.243, 46.313, 46.359, 46.406, 46.452, 46.534, 46.58, 46.626, 46.673, 46.719, 46.754, 46.801, 46.824, 46.847, 46.87, 46.917, 46.952, 47.01, 47.068, 47.137, 47.219, 47.277, 47.358, 47.404, 47.462, 47.532, 47.602, 47.671, 47.753, 47.822, 47.88, 47.95, 48.008, 48.054, 48.147, 48.263, 48.321, 48.496, 48.577, 49.088]}, "text": "At dawn the sheep file out. Odysseus has tied them in threes, with a man hanging under each middle one.\n\nHe hangs under the best ram himself. The blinded giant feels along their backs.\n\nTheir backs. Not their bellies.\n\n\"My good ram. You are always first out. Why are you last today? Are you sorry for your master's eye?\" the giant said softly.\n\nThe most sincere conversation in the poem.\n\nOdysseus should sail away quietly. He has won.\n\nInstead, he jeers at the giant from the deck. The giant tears the top off a mountain and throws it.\n\nIt lands just in front of the ship, a whisker from the rudder, and the wave carries the ship back toward shore."}